This is more of a style choice here, but i am a really big hater of mutation. I prefer if we can make things as functional as possible and never mutate variables and use reducers and other sutff.

Here is an example of using the same type of builder method but more functional (notice how we have all `const` variables)
```typesccript
export function buildProviderUrl(
  provider: Provider,
  modelString?: string,
  providerSettings?: {
    region?: string;
    project?: string;
    location?: string;
    endpoint?: string;
    deployment?: string;
  }
): string {
  const config = getProviderConfig(provider);

  // Define all possible replacements in a structured way
  const replacements = [
    { placeholder: /{modelString}/g, value: modelString },
    { placeholder: /{REGION}/g, value: providerSettings?.region },
    { placeholder: /{PROJECT}/g, value: providerSettings?.project },
    { placeholder: /{LOCATION}/g, value: providerSettings?.location },
    { placeholder: /{ENDPOINT}/g, value: providerSettings?.endpoint },
    { placeholder: /{DEPLOYMENT}/g, value: providerSettings?.deployment },
  ];

  // Apply replacements to baseUrl
  const processedBaseUrl = replacements.reduce(
    (url, { placeholder, value }) =>
      value ? url.replace(placeholder, value) : url,
    config.baseUrl
  );

  // Apply replacements to endpoint (excluding endpoint-specific ones)
  const processedEndpoint = replacements
    .filter(({ placeholder }) => placeholder.toString() !== "/{ENDPOINT}/g")
    .reduce(
      (endpoint, { placeholder, value }) =>
        value ? endpoint.replace(placeholder, value) : endpoint,
      config.defaultEndpoint || ""
    );

  // Final URL
  const finalUrl = `${processedBaseUrl}${processedEndpoint}`;

  // Safety check for any remaining placeholders
  if (finalUrl.includes("{") && finalUrl.includes("}")) {
    console.warn(`Warning: URL still contains placeholders: ${finalUrl}`);
  }

  return finalUrl;
}

```

With functional code, it is typically less error prone, easier to maintain IMO
